### [get_domain()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label .doc-label-method}

::: {.doc-subject}

Get the metadata of a dataset by name.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/posit-dev/pointblank/blob/main/pointblank/metadata/_types.py#L1267-L1307){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
get_domain(name)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

This does the same as `package[name]`, but a missing name gives an error message that
lists the names in the package.
:::

#### Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[name]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   The name of the dataset, such as `"DM"` or `"AE"`.
:::

#### Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [MetadataImport]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   The [`MetadataImport`](`pointblank.MetadataImport`) of the dataset.
:::

#### Raises {.doc-raises}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [KeyError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If there's no dataset with that name.
:::

#### Examples {.doc-examples}

::: {#42165b5f .cell execution_count=4}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import pointblank as pb

package = pb.import_metadata(pb.load_metadata_example("define.xml"))
ae = package.get_domain("AE")

print(ae.dataset_label)
print(ae.variable_names)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}
```
Adverse Events
['STUDYID', 'DOMAIN', 'USUBJID', 'AESEQ', 'AETERM', 'AEDECOD', 'AESEV', 'AESER', 'AESTDTC', 'AEENDTC']
```
:::
:::


Asking for a dataset that isn't in the package, such as `package.get_domain("LB")`, raises
a `KeyError` with the message `No domain/dataset named 'LB'. Available: ['DM', 'AE']`.

### [keys()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label .doc-label-method}

::: {.doc-subject}

Get the names of the datasets in the package.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/posit-dev/pointblank/blob/main/pointblank/metadata/_types.py#L1218-L1239){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
keys()
```
:::

#### Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [KeysView]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   The names, in the order of the source file, as a dictionary keys view.
:::

#### Examples {.doc-examples}

::: {#880aec32 .cell execution_count=5}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import pointblank as pb

ct = pb.import_metadata(pb.load_metadata_example("sdtm_ct.xml"))

list(ct.keys())
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=5}
```
['Sex',
 'Severity/Intensity Scale for Adverse Events',
 'No Yes Response',
 'Race',
 'Route of Administration']
```
:::
:::


For a Controlled Terminology file the entries are codelists, so the names are codelist
names. Each of them can be used to get an entry with `ct[name]`.

### [values()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label .doc-label-method}

::: {.doc-subject}

Get the metadata of the datasets in the package.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/posit-dev/pointblank/blob/main/pointblank/metadata/_types.py#L1241-L1265){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
values()
```
:::

#### Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [ValuesView]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   The [`MetadataImport`](`pointblank.MetadataImport`) entries, in the order of the source
    file, as a dictionary values view.
:::

#### Examples {.doc-examples}

::: {#4762fdf8 .cell execution_count=6}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import pointblank as pb

package = pb.import_metadata(pb.load_metadata_example("define.xml"))

for meta in package.values():
    print(meta.dataset_name, len(meta), list(meta.codelists))
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}
```
DM 13 ['Sex', 'Race']
AE 10 ['Severity', 'No Yes Response']
```
:::
:::


This is the easiest way to do something with every dataset, such as counting variables or
listing codelists as here. Use `package.items.items()` to get the names and entries
together.

### [summary()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label .doc-label-method}

::: {.doc-subject}

Get a text summary of the package.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/posit-dev/pointblank/blob/main/pointblank/metadata/_types.py#L1309-L1345){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
summary()
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

The summary gives the package's name and description (when known), the number of
datasets, and the number of variables in each. Printing a `MetadataPackage` with `print()`
shows the same summary. For the details of a dataset, use the
[`summary()`](`pointblank.MetadataImport.summary`) of its entry.
:::

#### Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [str]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   The summary, as a string with one line per item.
:::

#### Examples {.doc-examples}

::: {#15c4a564 .cell execution_count=7}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import pointblank as pb

package = pb.import_metadata(pb.load_metadata_example("define.xml"))

print(package.summary())
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}
```
Metadata Package
  Name: STUDY.XYZ789
  Description: CDISC Define-XML 2.0 study metadata
  Datasets: 2

  [DM] 13 variables
  [AE] 10 variables
```
:::
:::


The package is named after the study in the Define-XML document, and holds 13 variables
for the `DM` dataset and 10 for `AE`.
